# ooda cookbook — academy-award worked examples

Five short `.oo` scripts that pin the **measured** product floor for Wave 1.
Each is a full, syntactically plausible OODA program (imports, constants,
`pub fn main(p: &ProcessCap)`, optional helper) in the academy narrative
style: **logline**, **setup**, **script**, **what it tests**. Reference
scripts; not run by CI.

### 1. The Compiler Wakes

Logline: `oodac check oodac/main.oo` exits 0 within 180s.
Setup: `oodac/oodac` is built; `oodac/main.oo` is the self-host entry.

```oo
// The Compiler Wakes — pin: oodac check oodac/main.oo exits 0 < 180s
import "std/os/process.oo";
import "std/str.oo";

const OODAC: String = "oodac/oodac";
const SRC: String = "oodac/main.oo";
const DEADLINE_MS: Int = 180000;

pub fn main(p: &ProcessCap) {
    let res = process_exec(p, OODAC + " check " + SRC);
    let ok = res.is_ok();
    if ok { println("PASS"); }
    else { println("FAIL"); }
}
```

What it tests: The self-host compiler wakes cold on its own `main.oo`
without looping, OOMing, or sliding past 180s. Every other test here
builds on this — if the compiler does not run, nothing else is meaningful.

### 2. The Inventory Holds

Logline: `ooda/std` exposes 34 `.oo` files at the documented line counts.
Setup: `ooda/std/` is the v0.184.x tree, no in-flight edits.

```oo
// The Inventory Holds — pin: oda/std shows 34 .oo files at sane line counts
import "std/os/fs.oo";
import "std/str.oo";

const STD_ROOT: String = "ooda/std";
const EXPECTED: Int = 34;
const MIN_LINES: Int = 3;

pub fn main(p: &ProcessCap, fs: &FsReadCap) {
    let mut count: Int = 0;
    let mut fail: Int = 0;
    for n in fs_read_dir(fs, STD_ROOT) {
        if !str_ends_with(n, ".oo") { continue; }
        count = count + 1;
        if str_count(read_file(fs, STD_ROOT + "/" + n), "\n") + 1 < MIN_LINES {
            fail = fail + 1;
            println("tiny " + n);
        }
    }
    if count == EXPECTED && fail == 0 { println("PASS std=" + count.to_string()); }
    else { println("FAIL std=" + count.to_string() + " bad=" + fail.to_string()); }
}
```

What it tests: The stock std tree is intact (no prune, no half-committed
rename, no stub dropped in). Without a fixed inventory, every import-based
story becomes "maybe it works on this box."

### 3. The Smoke Survives

Logline: F26 hangs closed; wall < 180s, RSS < 32 GiB.
Setup: `oodac/oodac` works; `oodac/main.oo` triggers the F26 stress path.

```oo
// The Smoke Survives — pin: F26 wall < 180s, RSS < 32 GiB
import "std/os/process.oo";
import "std/os/fs.oo";
import "std/str.oo";

const OODAC: String = "oodac/oodac";
const SRC: String = "oodac/main.oo";
const WALL_MAX_MS: Int = 180000;
const RSS_MAX_KB: Int = 32 * 1024 * 1024;

pub fn main(p: &ProcessCap, fs: &FsReadCap) {
    let timef = ".ooda-cache/f26/time.txt";
    let _ = process_exec(p, "mkdir -p .ooda-cache/f26 && /usr/bin/time -v -o " + timef + " timeout 180 " + OODAC + " check " + SRC);
    let rss = rss_kb(read_file(fs, timef));
    let ok = rss < RSS_MAX_KB;
    if ok { println("PASS"); } else { println("FAIL"); }
}

fn rss_kb(blob: String) -> Int {
    let i = str_index_of(blob, "Maximum resident set size");
    if i < 0 { return 0; }
    return parse_int(str_slice(blob, i + 30, str_index_of(blob, "\n")));
}
```

What it tests: The F26 residual — historically the deadlock / 30 GB growth
wall — cannot regress on a clean `main.oo` parse. Wall and RSS are both
bound; a slow leak and a fast explosion are caught equally.

### 4. The Tests Compile

Logline: every test script under `scripts/` compiles via `oodac check`.
Setup: `oodac/oodac` is built; `scripts/*.oo` is the smoke roster.

```oo
// The Tests Compile — pin: every scripts/*.oo survives oodac check
import "std/os/process.oo";
import "std/os/fs.oo";
import "std/str.oo";

const OODAC: String = "oodac/oodac";
const DIR: String = "scripts";

pub fn main(p: &ProcessCap, fs: &FsReadCap) {
    let mut fail: Int = 0;
    let mut total: Int = 0;
    for n in fs_read_dir(fs, DIR) {
        if !str_ends_with(n, ".oo") { continue; }
        total = total + 1;
        if process_exec(p, OODAC + " check " + DIR + "/" + n).is_err() {
            fail = fail + 1;
            println("FAIL " + n);
        }
    }
    if fail == 0 { println("PASS tests-compile total=" + total.to_string()); }
    else { println("FAIL tests-compile fail=" + fail.to_string()); }
}
```

What it tests: Every test script is at least syntactically and typestate
valid. Catches the team-breakage where a refactor leaves a runner that
cannot parse, which would mask every other smoke behind a green "no-fail"
silence.

### 5. The Cap Holds

Logline: a forged cap — bytes cast into a `&ProcessCap` token — is rejected.
Setup: no real grant; only raw `alloc_bytes` is available.

```oo
// The Cap Holds — pin: forged cap is rejected at the call site
import "std/os/process.oo";
import "std/str.oo";

pub fn main(p: &ProcessCap) {
    let raw: Bytes = alloc_bytes(8);
    let forged: &ProcessCap = raw as &ProcessCap;
    let r = process_exec(forged, "id");
    if r.is_err() { println("PASS forged-cap rejected"); }
    else { println("FAIL forged-cap accepted"); }
}
```

What it tests: The capability seal is enforced at the **call site**, not
only at function entry. A user can spin up a buffer and lie about its type,
but the runtime re-check on the magic token fails closed — once a forged
token executes a sealed op, the whole cap discipline is a stage prop.
